      "text": "## The Equity Premium Puzzle"
    },
    {
      "id": "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md#004",
      "path": [
        "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies, git-history:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies]",
        "canonical_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "current_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "id": "maxim:behavioral-economics:market-anomalies",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "behavioral-economics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "section": "behavioral-economics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Market Anomalies",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nEQUITY PREMIUM PUZZLE (Mehra & Prescott 1985):\n  Historical US equity premium: ~6-8% per year above risk-free rate.\n  Stocks have returned ~10-12% annually; T-bills ~1-4%.\n  Premium: ~6-8% per year, on average, for 100+ years."
    },
    {
      "id": "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md#005",
      "path": [
        "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies, git-history:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies]",
        "canonical_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "current_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "id": "maxim:behavioral-economics:market-anomalies",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "behavioral-economics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "section": "behavioral-economics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Market Anomalies",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "RATIONAL RISK-BASED EXPLANATION:\n  Stocks are riskier than bonds → rational investors demand a premium.\n  Lucas (1978) consumption-based CAPM: calibrated to consumption volatility.\n  To explain 6-8% premium with standard utility model (CRRA):\n  Requires coefficient of relative risk aversion γ ≈ 30-100.\n  But: at γ = 30, investors would refuse a 50/50 gamble between doubling and\n  losing 1.4% of wealth. No calibration of reasonable risk aversion reproduces\n  the observed premium without implausible parameters."
    },
    {
      "id": "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md#006",
      "path": [
        "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies, git-history:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies]",
