        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "BEHAVIORAL EXPLANATIONS:"
    },
    {
      "id": "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md#007",
      "path": [
        "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies, git-history:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies]",
        "canonical_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "current_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "id": "maxim:behavioral-economics:market-anomalies",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "behavioral-economics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "section": "behavioral-economics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Market Anomalies",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "1. MYOPIC LOSS AVERSION (Benartzi & Thaler 1995):\n     Combines loss aversion (λ ≈ 2.25) with narrow framing (evaluate portfolio\n     performance frequently — myopically).\n     If investors evaluate losses annually: stocks look scary (20% chance of loss\n     in any given year → loss aversion pain).\n     If evaluated over 10-year horizon: much less often a loss year.\n     Calibrated model with λ = 2.25 and annual evaluation:\n     Exactly reproduces the historical ~6% equity premium!\n     This is among the most successful quantitative behavioral predictions."
    },
    {
      "id": "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md#008",
      "path": [
        "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies, git-history:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies]",
        "canonical_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "current_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "id": "maxim:behavioral-economics:market-anomalies",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "behavioral-economics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "section": "behavioral-economics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Market Anomalies",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "2. HABIT FORMATION (Campbell & Cochrane 1999):\n     Risk premium depends on local risk aversion, which varies with wealth\n     relative to habit level. When wealth falls near habit: extreme aversion.\n     Captures time variation in risk premium."
    },
    {
      "id": "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md#009",
      "path": [
        "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies, git-history:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies]",
        "canonical_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "current_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "id": "maxim:behavioral-economics:market-anomalies",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "behavioral-economics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "section": "behavioral-economics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Market Anomalies",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "3. AMBIGUITY AVERSION:\n     Equity returns involve Knightian uncertainty (unknown probability distribution).\n     Ambiguity aversion → additional premium demanded over and above risk premium."
    },
    {
      "id": "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md#010",
      "path": [
        "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies, git-history:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies]",
        "canonical_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "current_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "id": "maxim:behavioral-economics:market-anomalies",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "behavioral-economics",
