      "text": "## Disposition Effect"
    },
    {
      "id": "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md#012",
      "path": [
        "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies, git-history:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies]",
        "canonical_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "current_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "id": "maxim:behavioral-economics:market-anomalies",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "behavioral-economics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "section": "behavioral-economics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Market Anomalies",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nDISPOSITION EFFECT (Shefrin & Statman 1985):\n  Investors tend to:\n  SELL winners too early  (realize gains quickly)\n  HOLD losers too long    (avoid realizing losses)"
    },
    {
      "id": "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md#013",
      "path": [
        "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies, git-history:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies]",
        "canonical_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "current_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "id": "maxim:behavioral-economics:market-anomalies",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "behavioral-economics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "section": "behavioral-economics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Market Anomalies",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "MECHANISM: Prospect Theory prediction.\n  Reference point = purchase price.\n  When price > purchase price: gain domain. Concave utility → risk averse → sell\n    to lock in gain (avoid chance of going back down).\n  When price < purchase price: loss domain. Convex utility → risk seeking → hold\n    to avoid locking in the loss (gamble on recovery)."
    },
    {
      "id": "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md#014",
      "path": [
        "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies, git-history:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies]",
        "canonical_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "current_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "id": "maxim:behavioral-economics:market-anomalies",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "behavioral-economics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "section": "behavioral-economics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Market Anomalies",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "EMPIRICAL EVIDENCE:\n  Odean (1998): analysis of 10,000 individual brokerage accounts (1987-1993).\n  \"Proportion of gains realized\" / \"Proportion of losses realized\":\n  PGR = 0.148 (sell ~15% of winning positions)\n  PLR = 0.098 (sell ~10% of losing positions)\n  Disposition ratio = PGR/PLR = 1.51 (50% more likely to sell winners than losers)\n  Robust: persists after controlling for transaction costs, taxes, portfolio rebalancing."
    },
    {
      "id": "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md#015",
      "path": [
        "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies, git-history:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies]",
        "canonical_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "current_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "id": "maxim:behavioral-economics:market-anomalies",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "behavioral-economics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "section": "behavioral-economics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Market Anomalies",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "TAX IMPLICATION: The rational strategy is exactly OPPOSITE.\n  For taxable accounts: sell losers at year-end (realize capital losses → tax benefit).\n  Hold winners (defer capital gains → delay tax payment).\n  Investors are doing the wrong thing for tax purposes, consistent with loss aversion."
    },
    {
      "id": "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md#016",
      "path": [
        "08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies, git-history:behavioral-economics:08-market-anomalies]",
        "canonical_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "current_path": "behavioral-economics/08-MARKET-ANOMALIES.md",
        "id": "maxim:behavioral-economics:market-anomalies",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "behavioral-economics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[market, anomalies]",
        "section": "behavioral-economics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Market Anomalies",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "AGGREGATE MARKET IMPLICATIONS:\n  If many investors hold losers and sell winners:\n  Past winners → selling pressure → underperform going forward\n  Past losers → holding (limited selling pressure) → underperform due to continued drift\n  This partially explains the MOMENTUM anomaly (below)."
    }
